--TEST--
`Core\Router::url` writes an enum case as the segment the match claims — the written backing value where every admitted case wrote one, and the case name where any counted — `rule:routing/an-enum-capture-is-spelled-by-its-backing-value-or-its-case-name`, `rule:routing/link-name-and-params-are-checked`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Both halves of the rule in one file, because the spelling is decided per
// subset and each half only reads as a choice against the other. `Lang` wrote
// both values, so a segment is that integer. `Shelf` counts its cases on from
// zero, so a segment is the case name: a counted value is a position, and a
// link built out of `/s/0` would name a different case the day a case is
// inserted ahead of `New`.
enum Lang: uint { De = 7, En = 9 }
enum Shelf { New, Sale }

class Docs {
    // `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling`: the decision is a required sibling of every `#[Route]`.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/p/{lang}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "docs.page")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function page(Lang $lang): string { return "page"; }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/s/{shelf}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "docs.shelf")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function shelf(Shelf $shelf): string { return "shelf"; }
}

// A case written at the call site, under each of the two spellings.
echo "written=", Core\Router::url("docs.page", ["lang" => Lang::De]), "\n";
echo "counted=", Core\Router::url("docs.shelf", ["shelf" => Shelf::Sale]), "\n";

// A case the compile-time pass never read, because it is held in a variable.
// The conversion is the link's own rather than the call site's, so a computed
// case builds the segment a written one builds — which is what makes the
// spelling a property of the route and not of how the program reached it.
var $held = Shelf::New;
echo "computed=", Core\Router::url("docs.shelf", ["shelf" => $held]), "\n";

// A case is its backing integer by the time it is a value
// (`rule:enums/no-class-machinery`), so a program writing the segment itself
// reaches the same link: the rows the link carries convert a value and leave a
// segment alone.
echo "spelled=", Core\Router::url("docs.shelf", ["shelf" => "Sale"]), "\n";

// The agreement the spelling exists for, counted rather than read off a line:
// every admitted case has exactly one link, and it is the segment this route's
// own matcher claims. A link that wrote the backing integer under the counted
// spelling moves this number without moving any single expectation above.
array<string> $segments = ["New", "Sale"];
array<string> $built = [
    Core\Router::url("docs.shelf", ["shelf" => Shelf::New]),
    Core\Router::url("docs.shelf", ["shelf" => Shelf::Sale]),
];
var $agree = 0;
foreach ($segments as string $segment) {
    foreach ($built as string $link) {
        if ($link == "/s/" . $segment) {
            $agree = $agree + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "agree=", $agree as string, "/", Core\Arr::count($segments) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
written=/p/7
counted=/s/Sale
computed=/s/New
spelled=/s/Sale
agree=2/2
